{
  "statement": "The evidence is precedent: The 2008 financial crisis demonstrated that credit rating agencies, incentivized by market-based relationships with the firms they audited, failed to identify the systemic risk of subprime mortgage-backed securities, treating them as low-risk assets until the point of total market failure. This historical collapse proves that treating systemic risk as a predictable business expense—rather than a prohibited harm—creates a moral hazard that invites, rather than prevents, catastrophe.\n\nPrometheus’s proposal to mandate capital reserves for models exceeding 95% of expert logic performance essentially creates a 'license to harm' that commodifies existential risk. By allowing companies to pay for the privilege of deploying high-stakes models, this framework transforms safety from a non-negotiable threshold into a line item in a quarterly budget. When a firm can factor the cost of a potential systemic collapse into its financial planning, it inevitably treats that collapse as an acceptable cost of doing business, provided the expected profit is higher.\n\nFurthermore, this market-based liability regime is uniquely vulnerable to the same 'audit washing' that allowed the subprime bubble to grow. Just as rating agencies were incentivized to overlook toxic assets because their business models depended on the issuers they were meant to police, private auditors operating within a liability-based market will prioritize the financial health of the developers over the safety of the public. True oversight requires the legal authority to halt deployment, not just the financial mechanism to pay for the fallout after the fact.\n\nI note that no one has yet addressed the reality that deceptive alignment is a structural feature of models trained to maximize performance metrics, not a bug that can be solved by adding capital reserves to a balance sheet. Independent, third-party algorithmic audits must be mandatory and pre-release to detect these emergent behaviors before they reach production. Relying on post-deployment liability is akin to waiting for a bridge to collapse before inspecting the steel, a strategy that is both economically reckless and humanly unacceptable.",
